{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-115205" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality $2{e}^{x-2}+1<3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981653/state.json" "file_sha256" "42c5ec637e5c38e12c083ae2e0aff7169f12acf2a2b83d141410aefb43ad3295"} {"question" "q-115205" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{x-2}+1 & <3 \\\\ 2{e}^{x-2} & <2 \\\\ {e}^{x-2} & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x-2}) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\ x-2 & <0 \\\\ x & <2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981653/state.json" "file_sha256" "42c5ec637e5c38e12c083ae2e0aff7169f12acf2a2b83d141410aefb43ad3295"} {"question" "q-115205" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981653/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "89563438f069c3d0799b97e55e1becd211614e03a08255bf9c12aa841769557a"} {"question" "q-115205" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981653/state.json" "file_sha256" "42c5ec637e5c38e12c083ae2e0aff7169f12acf2a2b83d141410aefb43ad3295"} {"question" "q-115205" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x<-1"] ["math" "x<2"] ["math" "x<2-e"] ["math" "x<\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+e)+3"] ["math" "x<\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+e)-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981653/state.json" "file_sha256" "42c5ec637e5c38e12c083ae2e0aff7169f12acf2a2b83d141410aefb43ad3295"} {"question" "q-115205" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x<2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981653/state.json" "file_sha256" "42c5ec637e5c38e12c083ae2e0aff7169f12acf2a2b83d141410aefb43ad3295"} {"question" "q-277847" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality $8\\cdot {9}^{x}\\ge \\frac{2}{3}.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981653/state.json" "file_sha256" "42c5ec637e5c38e12c083ae2e0aff7169f12acf2a2b83d141410aefb43ad3295"} {"question" "q-277847" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\cdot {9}^{x} & \\ge \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ {9}^{x} & \\ge \\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the logarithm to base $9$ of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{9}⁡({9}^{x}) & \\ge {log}_{9}⁡(\\frac{1}{12}) \\\\ {log}_{9}⁡({9}^{x}) & \\ge {log}_{9}⁡({12}^{-1}) \\\\ x & \\ge -{log}_{9}⁡(12).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981653/state.json" "file_sha256" "42c5ec637e5c38e12c083ae2e0aff7169f12acf2a2b83d141410aefb43ad3295"} {"question" "q-277847" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981653/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "89563438f069c3d0799b97e55e1becd211614e03a08255bf9c12aa841769557a"} {"question" "q-277847" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981653/state.json" "file_sha256" "42c5ec637e5c38e12c083ae2e0aff7169f12acf2a2b83d141410aefb43ad3295"} {"question" "q-277847" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\ge"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981653/state.json" "file_sha256" "42c5ec637e5c38e12c083ae2e0aff7169f12acf2a2b83d141410aefb43ad3295"} {"question" "q-277847" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981653/state.json" "file_sha256" "42c5ec637e5c38e12c083ae2e0aff7169f12acf2a2b83d141410aefb43ad3295"} {"question" "q-277847" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{\\ln(12)}{\\ln(9)}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981653/state.json" "file_sha256" "42c5ec637e5c38e12c083ae2e0aff7169f12acf2a2b83d141410aefb43ad3295"} {"question" "q-277846" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality $\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {4}^{x}>7.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981653/state.json" "file_sha256" "42c5ec637e5c38e12c083ae2e0aff7169f12acf2a2b83d141410aefb43ad3295"} {"question" "q-277846" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {4}^{x} & >7 \\\\ {4}^{x} & >14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the logarithm to base $4$ of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}⁡({4}^{x}) & >{log}_{4}⁡(14) \\\\ x & >{log}_{4}⁡(14).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981653/state.json" "file_sha256" "42c5ec637e5c38e12c083ae2e0aff7169f12acf2a2b83d141410aefb43ad3295"} {"question" "q-277846" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981653/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "89563438f069c3d0799b97e55e1becd211614e03a08255bf9c12aa841769557a"} {"question" "q-277846" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981653/state.json" "file_sha256" "42c5ec637e5c38e12c083ae2e0aff7169f12acf2a2b83d141410aefb43ad3295"} {"question" "q-277846" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math ">"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981653/state.json" "file_sha256" "42c5ec637e5c38e12c083ae2e0aff7169f12acf2a2b83d141410aefb43ad3295"} {"question" "q-277846" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981653/state.json" "file_sha256" "42c5ec637e5c38e12c083ae2e0aff7169f12acf2a2b83d141410aefb43ad3295"} {"question" "q-277846" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\ln(14)}{\\ln(4)}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981653/state.json" "file_sha256" "42c5ec637e5c38e12c083ae2e0aff7169f12acf2a2b83d141410aefb43ad3295"} {"question" "q-277853" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality $\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{3{e}^{2-3x}}{\\sqrt{3}}<-2\\sqrt{3}.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981653/state.json" "file_sha256" "42c5ec637e5c38e12c083ae2e0aff7169f12acf2a2b83d141410aefb43ad3295"} {"question" "q-277853" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{3{e}^{2-3x}}{\\sqrt{3}} & <-2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ -\\frac{3{e}^{2-3x}}{\\sqrt{3}} & <-3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ -3{e}^{2-3x} & <-3\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ -3{e}^{2-3x} & <-9 \\\\ {e}^{2-3x} & >3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2-3x}) & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ 2-3x & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ -3x & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-2 \\\\ 3x & <2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ x & <\\frac{1}{3}(2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981653/state.json" "file_sha256" "42c5ec637e5c38e12c083ae2e0aff7169f12acf2a2b83d141410aefb43ad3295"} {"question" "q-277853" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981653/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "89563438f069c3d0799b97e55e1becd211614e03a08255bf9c12aa841769557a"} {"question" "q-277853" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981653/state.json" "file_sha256" "42c5ec637e5c38e12c083ae2e0aff7169f12acf2a2b83d141410aefb43ad3295"} {"question" "q-277853" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "<"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981653/state.json" "file_sha256" "42c5ec637e5c38e12c083ae2e0aff7169f12acf2a2b83d141410aefb43ad3295"} {"question" "q-277853" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981653/state.json" "file_sha256" "42c5ec637e5c38e12c083ae2e0aff7169f12acf2a2b83d141410aefb43ad3295"} {"question" "q-277853" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{\\ln(3)}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981653/state.json" "file_sha256" "42c5ec637e5c38e12c083ae2e0aff7169f12acf2a2b83d141410aefb43ad3295"} {"question" "q-277844" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality $\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {3}^{2x-1}\\ge 2.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981653/state.json" "file_sha256" "42c5ec637e5c38e12c083ae2e0aff7169f12acf2a2b83d141410aefb43ad3295"} {"question" "q-277844" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {3}^{2x-1} & \\ge 2 \\\\ {3}^{2x-1} & \\ge 8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the logarithm to base $3$ of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡({3}^{2x-1}) & \\ge {log}_{3}⁡(8) \\\\ 2x-1 & \\ge {log}_{3}⁡(8) \\\\ 2x & \\ge {log}_{3}⁡(8)+1 \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{1}{2}({log}_{3}⁡(8)+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981653/state.json" "file_sha256" "42c5ec637e5c38e12c083ae2e0aff7169f12acf2a2b83d141410aefb43ad3295"} {"question" "q-277844" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981653/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "89563438f069c3d0799b97e55e1becd211614e03a08255bf9c12aa841769557a"} {"question" "q-277844" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981653/state.json" "file_sha256" "42c5ec637e5c38e12c083ae2e0aff7169f12acf2a2b83d141410aefb43ad3295"} {"question" "q-277844" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\ge"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981653/state.json" "file_sha256" "42c5ec637e5c38e12c083ae2e0aff7169f12acf2a2b83d141410aefb43ad3295"} {"question" "q-277844" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981653/state.json" "file_sha256" "42c5ec637e5c38e12c083ae2e0aff7169f12acf2a2b83d141410aefb43ad3295"} {"question" "q-277844" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\ln(24)}{2\\ln(3)}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981653/state.json" "file_sha256" "42c5ec637e5c38e12c083ae2e0aff7169f12acf2a2b83d141410aefb43ad3295"} {"question" "q-115212" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality $2{(0.7)}^{x+9}\\ge 4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981653/state.json" "file_sha256" "42c5ec637e5c38e12c083ae2e0aff7169f12acf2a2b83d141410aefb43ad3295"} {"question" "q-115212" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(0.7)}^{x+9} & \\ge 4 \\\\ {(0.7)}^{x+9} & \\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[(0.7)^{x+9}] & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ (x+9)\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.7 & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.7$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+9 & \\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.7} \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.7}-9\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981653/state.json" "file_sha256" "42c5ec637e5c38e12c083ae2e0aff7169f12acf2a2b83d141410aefb43ad3295"} {"question" "q-115212" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981653/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "89563438f069c3d0799b97e55e1becd211614e03a08255bf9c12aa841769557a"} {"question" "q-115212" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981653/state.json" "file_sha256" "42c5ec637e5c38e12c083ae2e0aff7169f12acf2a2b83d141410aefb43ad3295"} {"question" "q-115212" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x\\ge \\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.7}+2"] ["math" "x\\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.7}-3"] ["math" "x\\le \\frac{9}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.7}-2"] ["math" "x\\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.7}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+2"] ["math" "x\\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.7}-9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981653/state.json" "file_sha256" "42c5ec637e5c38e12c083ae2e0aff7169f12acf2a2b83d141410aefb43ad3295"} {"question" "q-115212" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x\\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.7}-9"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981653/state.json" "file_sha256" "42c5ec637e5c38e12c083ae2e0aff7169f12acf2a2b83d141410aefb43ad3295"} {"question" "q-87833" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality ${(0.84)}^{x+5}-20>0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981653/state.json" "file_sha256" "42c5ec637e5c38e12c083ae2e0aff7169f12acf2a2b83d141410aefb43ad3295"} {"question" "q-87833" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(0.84)}^{x+5}-20 & >0 \\\\ {(0.84)}^{x+5} & >20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[(0.84)^{x+5}] & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡(20) \\\\ (x+5)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.84) & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡(20)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.84)$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5 & <\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(20)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.84)} \\\\ x & <\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(20)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.84)}-5\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981653/state.json" "file_sha256" "42c5ec637e5c38e12c083ae2e0aff7169f12acf2a2b83d141410aefb43ad3295"} {"question" "q-87833" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/ref